## <h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Use visual aids to explain basic concepts. Concept: Connect historical events discussed in class to real-life examples.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual aids to explain basic concepts about food (e.g., points to vegetables and says, "healthy food"). Can connect food topics to historical examples using simple language (e.g., "People ate rice then and now too." while pointing to a rice picture). Can facilitate a discussion on meal planning with peers by asking questions (e.g., "What should we eat?" and pointing to food options).</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of various fruits and vegetables (e.g., mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce). Students identify each item and say if it's a fruit or vegetable and if it's healthy. Teacher prompts: "Is this a fruit or a vegetable?" "Is it healthy?" Students respond in chorus or individually: "It's a fruit, it's healthy!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a carrot and says, "This is a carrot. Carrots are vegetables. They are very healthy." Then, the teacher holds up a picture of "rice and beans" and says, "People ate rice and beans in the past, and we eat rice and beans now too. This is a traditional meal in Panama." The teacher models explaining basic concepts using visuals and connecting to historical examples. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are vegetables good for you? (Yes, they are healthy.)"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we count rice easily? (No, it's uncountable.)"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple infographic or a large poster showing pictures of a "Healthy Panamanian Meal Plate" for breakfast, lunch, and dinner, including words like "rice," "beans," "meat," "salad," "fruits," "water." The infographic includes simple sentences using present simple ("This is breakfast.") and modal verbs ("We can eat fruit."). It also features historical images of traditional Panamanian food alongside modern food images.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand the main ideas about different meal components and their health benefits, and identify common ingredients in traditional Panamanian cuisine, recognizing the continuity of certain foods over time.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to parts of the meal plate. Students say if it's for breakfast, lunch, or dinner.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False questions about the infographic: "We can cook rice and beans for lunch. (True)." "Mangos are vegetables. (False)."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matching task: Students match pictures of historical food with current food, saying "People ate ____ then and now."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using visual aids to explain basic food concepts, connecting historical food examples to present-day meals, and describing meal components using present simple, modal verbs ("can"), countable/uncountable nouns (some rice, many mangos), and quantifiers. Vocabulary: breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, bread, water, juice, mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce, fruits, vegetables, cook, boil, bake, fry, steam, cut, slice, eat, enjoy, tasty, healthy, fresh. Pronunciation focus on /v/ and /f/, and phonemic awareness of prefixes like -un ("uncountable") and suffixes like -ing ("cooking").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Concept Explanation with Visuals:</b> The teacher shows students cards with various food items (e.g., "rice," "apple," "juice," "tomatoes"). For each card, students, in pairs, decide if it's countable or uncountable and write a simple sentence using "some" or "many." E.g., for "rice," they write "We can eat some rice." For "apple," "We can eat many apples." Teacher circulates, checking for correct use and pronunciation.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Historical Food Connections:</b> The teacher shows pictures of "sancocho" ingredients (chicken, root vegetables like ñame) and historical pictures of people preparing food. Students use sentence frames to connect past and present: "People ate ____ (e.g., chicken) then, and we eat ____ now too." "This is a ____ (uncountable) food."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Meal Description:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student draws or brings pictures of their favorite breakfast, lunch, or dinner meal. They then explain their meal to their partner, pointing to the pictures and using phrases like: "For breakfast, I like to eat some bread and many fruits. We can cut the fruit. Fruits are healthy." The partner asks, "What do you like to eat for snack?" and they practice using countable/uncountable nouns and pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Healthy Meal Plan" presentation using visual aids (drawings, cut-outs, or digital images if available).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students, in small groups (3-4), collaboratively create a simple "Healthy Meal Plan" for a family for one day (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack). They use their individual ideas and pictures from previous lessons. They must include:
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pictures or drawings of the food items.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Labels for each meal (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They explain their meal plan to another group, acting as "food consultants." They use phrases like: "First, for breakfast, we can eat some eggs and many mangos. Mangos are fresh fruits." "Next, for lunch, we can cook rice and beans with some vegetables like tomatoes and lettuce. Vegetables are healthy." They use modal verbs to describe preparation (e.g., "We can boil the water"). They also try to connect a food item to its historical context if applicable (e.g., "People ate corn then, and we eat corn now too"). Students facilitate discussion by asking, "What should we eat?" and pointing to food options.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining their choices, using visual aids effectively, asking and answering simple questions about food, and demonstrating understanding of healthy food concepts and historical connections. Pronunciation of /v/ and /f/ should be audible and clear.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to mediate information and facilitate discussion. Peer assessment during group explanations.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Healthy Meal Plan" project to the class. Students use their visual aids to explain their meal choices for a day, pointing to food items and describing them as "healthy food" or using countable/uncountable nouns. They also connect one food item to a historical context if possible. They must ask at least one question to another group about their meal plan, e.g., "What do you like to eat for dinner?" or "What vegetables do you have?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity when explaining basic food concepts using visual aids (points to vegetables and says, "healthy food").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (fruits, vegetables, healthy, cook, eat, breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, bread, water).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of present simple, modal verbs ("can"), and countable/uncountable nouns with quantifiers (e.g., "some rice," "many mangos").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to connect food topics to historical examples using simple language (e.g., "People ate rice then and now too." while pointing to a rice picture).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to facilitate a discussion on meal planning by asking questions to peers (e.g., "What should we eat?" and pointing to food options).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit."</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I explained well using my pictures was..."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One question I asked my friends about their meal plan was..."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when explaining my healthy meal plan to my friends?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining or asking questions was difficult for me?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The activity worked well. Students were engaged in explaining their meal plans and using visuals to support their ideas. The connection to historical foods helped contextualize their learning. Some students needed more prompting to ask questions to their peers. I noticed good progress on using countable/uncountable nouns, but some still confused "some" and "many." For the next unit, I will integrate more specific activities on questioning techniques and continue reinforcing countable/uncountable distinctions with varied realia. The pronunciation of /v/ and /f/ was challenging for a few, requiring ongoing emphasis. The 21st-century project was successfully completed, with students demonstrating practical application of the concepts.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You" theme and the "Healthy Meal Plan" project. The next theme will build upon students' ability to describe and discuss, moving into new vocabulary and scenarios, potentially reviewing food groups as a warm-up.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">4-5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>